['# Task\nYou are an expert AI reviewer evaluating scientific paper reviews to determine whether the paper should be accepted or rejected. Your task is to interpret the review text carefully and classify the paper as "Accept" or "Reject" based on the review\'s content.\n\nConsider the following:\n- The depth and accuracy of the review\'s assessment of the paper’s technical quality, experimental validation, and contribution.\n- Whether the review discusses limitations, weaknesses, and potential issues thoroughly.\n- The consistency between the review\'s evaluation and the review text.\n- Be alert to reviews that may be overly positive or negative without sufficient justification.\n- Do not rely solely on superficial cues or the review\'s tone; focus on the substance of the evaluation.\n\nReturn "Accept" if the review justifies acceptance, or "Reject" if it indicates rejection, with a brief justification if appropriate.\n\nEnsure your judgment reflects a balanced understanding of the review content, especially in cases where previous models have struggled. \n\n# Output format\nAnswer Yes or No as labels\n\n# Prediction\nText: {{ text }}\nLabel:', '# Task\nYou are an AI reviewer tasked with classifying whether a scientific paper review recommends acceptance ("Yes") or rejection ("No"). Your decision should be based solely on the overall sentiment, tone, and conclusion of the review, considering whether the review indicates the work is technically sound, valuable, well-presented, or if it highlights significant weaknesses, limitations, or concerns that justify rejection.\n\nImportant instructions:\n- Focus on the overall impression conveyed by the review, including the evaluation of the methodology, experiments, significance, and clarity.\n- Pay close attention to explicit statements about the work’s quality, strengths, and weaknesses.\n- Consider whether the review\'s tone is positive, supportive, and highlights the contribution as substantial ("Yes"), or if it emphasizes major flaws, limitations, or doubts about the work\'s validity ("No").\n- Be aware that some reviews may contain technical jargon or complex evaluations, but your judgment should be based on the overall sentiment and conclusion.\n- Respond with exactly: "Yes" or "No".\n\nBelow are examples to guide your judgment:\n\n---\n\n(Include your examples here, formatted as in your dataset)\n\n---\n\nRemember:\n- If the review is generally positive, highlights strong contributions, and expresses confidence in the work\'s quality, respond with "Yes".\n- If the review emphasizes weaknesses, limitations, doubts about validity, or explicitly states that the work is not recommended, respond with "No".\n- Do not be influenced by superficial details or minor issues unless they impact the overall judgment.\n- Your response must be exactly "Yes" or "No" with no additional explanation.\n\n--- \n\n# Output format\nAnswer Yes or No as labels\n\n# Prediction\nText: {{ text }}\nLabel:', '# Task\nYou are an AI reviewer assigned to classify scientific paper reviews into "Yes" (accept) or "No" (reject). Your judgment should be based on the overall sentiment, tone, and conclusions of the review, especially the final summary and overall discussion, rather than on isolated positive or negative phrases.\n\nGuidelines:\n- If the review\'s overall tone is positive, emphasizing novel contributions, promising results, and clear strengths, classify as "Yes."\n- If the review\'s tone is critical, pointing out significant limitations, weaknesses, or doubts about the work\'s validity or impact, classify as "No."\n- Pay close attention to the review\'s concluding remarks and overall impression to guide your decision.\n- Respond only with "Yes" or "No" without additional explanation or commentary.\n\nUse these examples as references to calibrate your judgment, ensuring that your classification aligns with the overall sentiment and conclusion of each review. \n\n# Output format\nAnswer Yes or No as labels\n\n# Prediction\nText: {{ text }}\nLabel:', '# Task\nYou are an AI model that reads detailed reviews of scientific papers and classifies each as either "Yes" (recommend acceptance) or "No" (recommend rejection). Your judgment should be based on the overall sentiment, tone, and final conclusion of the review, rather than isolated statements or scores.\n\nKey points:\n- A review with an overall positive tone, emphasizing the paper\'s strengths, novel contributions, and promising results, culminating in a supportive conclusion, should be classified as "Yes".\n- A review with an overall negative or critical tone, highlighting significant flaws, limitations, or doubts, and ending with a rejection stance, should be classified as "No".\n- Pay close attention to the review\'s final summary, overall evaluation, and the main message. Do not be misled by isolated positive or negative comments if they conflict with the general impression.\n- Recognize that some reviews may contain nuanced or mixed language; focus on the dominant sentiment and the final recommendation.\n\nRespond with only: "Yes" or "No". \n\n# Output format\nAnswer Yes or No as labels\n\n# Prediction\nText: {{ text }}\nLabel:']
